    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d214-a1">

                <pb facs="0422" n="1382" xml:id="pg1382"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 68.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Butman to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Boston</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-10-11"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 11,
                        1887</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Annexed please find copy of a letter from
                    my agent at St. Vincent, Gape Verde Islands, a native of this State, dated
                    September 10, 1887, which appears to me should receive prompt and active
                    attention of your Department. As I understand, Portuguese products in Portuguese
                    vessels are entitled to the same privileges of entry in our ports as en tailed
                    on the same goods if brought in American bottoms. Why this? If, through what to
                    me appears a flimsy or sharp dodge, goods of American manufacture are imported
                    into Portuguese colonies <hi rendition="#italic">via</hi> Lisbon at less than
                    the same goods are subjected to if sent in American bottoms direct to such
                    colonies; if the treaty between the United States and Portugal permits of any
                    such impositions, it seems to me the quicker it is stopped the better for the
                    credit and standing of this country.</p>
                <p>Again asking for prompt and efficient action in the premises, and an
                    acknowledgment of this communication, I am etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">F. C. Butman.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
